course: dictionary
lesson: 2621
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them, the context, show you which meaning the writer wants. Read the whole sentence, pick the meaning that fits best, then swap it in to check.
explain: Descent means going down. A plane begins its descent when it starts to come down to land, and a climber makes a descent when they come down a mountain. In both cases the height goes down.
explain: «Red-green colour blindness affects about 1 in 12 men of European descent.» Here descent does not mean going down. The clue is "of European descent", which describes people. It means family background: where a family's people came from. Swap in "going down" and the sentence stops making sense.
fact: Descent means a way or act of going down, like going down a hill, stairs or from the sky.
fact: Descent also means your family background: the people and places your family comes from.
fact: A descent can also be a slope that goes downward.
fact: Phrases to know: begin the descent, a steep descent, of Irish descent, and descent from.
fact: Descent is a noun, so you can write a descent or descents, and it can also be uncountable.
exercise: «The plane began its descent as it neared the airport.» What does descent mean here? | A kind of cake | Family background | *Going down towards the ground | Going up into the clouds
exercise: «Red-green colour blindness affects about 1 in 12 men of European descent.» What does descent mean here? | *Family background, where a family's people came from | Going up the stairs | Going down a steep hill | A deep sleep
exercise: Which phrase means a slope that goes steeply downward? | *a steep descent | a long river | a high mountain | a deep sleep
test: «The climber's descent of the mountain took three hours.» What does descent mean here? | Climbing up to the top | *Going down the mountain | Family background | A kind of slope in a garden
test: «She is of Irish descent.» What does descent mean here? | A steep hill she has climbed | *Family background, the country her family came from | Going down the stairs | A plane landing at the airport
exam: «Red-green colour blindness affects about 1 in 12 men of European descent.» Sam says descent always means going down. Is Sam right? | Yes, but only on stairs | No, it means a deep sleep | *No, here it means family background, so Sam is wrong | Yes, always, and this sentence proves it
